# Dataset Card: CBIS-DDSM full mammograms with radiologist mass masks (Phase 2)

## Overview
- **Tasks:**
  - detection of masses (one class, `mass`) on full mammograms (M4);
  - pixel-level segmentation of the same masses (M5);
  - benign vs. malignant classification of each found mass, reusing the Phase 1 classifier (M8).
- **Source:** CBIS-DDSM (Curated Breast Imaging Subset of DDSM; Lee et al., Scientific Data 2017; original collection on TCIA), JPEG version from the Kaggle mirror `awsaf49/cbis-ddsm-breast-cancer-image-dataset`, the same source as Phase 1.
- **Annotations:** the ROI masks drawn by radiologists in CBIS-DDSM; boxes are the bounding rectangles of these masks, polygons their outer contours. No labels were generated by a model.
- **Licence:** listed as CC-BY-SA-3.0 by the Kaggle CLI for this mirror. Verify the terms of the original TCIA collection before any redistribution. Images are **not** redistributed or committed to the repository.
- **Content:** mass cases only (official train / test CSVs). Calcification cases are left out: they are clusters of tiny dots, not single objects to box and outline.

## Composition after cleaning
| split | masses | images | patients | malignant % |
|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| train | 867 | 815 | 493 | 49.3 |
| val | 216 | 199 | 123 | 44.0 |
| test | 325 | 311 | 186 | 37.5 |

## Splits (patient-level, leakage-safe, as in Phase 1)
- Test = official CBIS-DDSM test set. Validation = 20 % of the training patients (seed 42).
- Train rows of patients that also appear in test are removed.
- Verified in code: no patient appears in more than one of train / val / test.

## Cleaning and conversion
- CSV rows read: 1,696. Excluded rows: 288 (mask_not_found_or_size_mismatch: 288).
- Full image and ROI mask linked through the DICOM series UID in the CSV paths (Phase 1 plan item 4). In the mirror, masks and cropped ROI images share a folder; the mask is taken as the file with the full-image size and (nearly) binary values. Rows without such a file are excluded and logged in `detection/excluded.csv`.
- Images resized so the long side is 1024 px; masks resized with nearest-neighbour.
- Converted: 1,408 masses on 1,325 images; 0 masks have several parts (box covers all parts, polygon uses the largest); 0 masks empty after resizing (dropped).

## Preprocessing and augmentation
- Detector / segmenter: 640 px input; training augmentation horizontal and vertical flip, brightness jitter, mosaic (closed for the last epochs); no rotation or hue change.
- Classifier: crop around the radiologist mask with 15 % margin, resized to 224 x 224, ImageNet normalisation; training augmentation flips and brightness scaling.

## Mask audit
- Random sample of 100 masses (seed 42) in `audit_sample.csv` with automatic checks per mask: number of parts, mask area, fill of its bounding box and share of mask pixels on breast tissue.
- `mask_parts` counts connected regions at full resolution, including small specks left by JPEG compression of the mask; the specks are dropped in conversion (regions under 16 px are ignored), so all converted masks have one part.
- A mask is flagged when it covers less than 0.005 % or more than 20 % of the image, or when less than 95 % of its pixels lie on breast tissue (grey level above 20); the 4 flagged masks lie on dark fatty tissue or at the breast edge.
- Automatic check result: 96 pass, 4 flagged.
- `figures/audit_sheet.png` shows every sampled mask on its image for visual review; the review columns are filled by the reviewer.

## Known limitations and biases
- Digitised film mammograms from a historical US collection; scanners, population and protocol may not match modern digital mammography.
- ROI outlines are loose and include margin around the mass, which limits pixel-level agreement.
- Masses are small relative to the full image; small and low-contrast masses are the hardest to find.
- One patient can contribute several images (views, masses), so metrics are correlated.
- Pathology labels come from the dataset (biopsy) and were not re-verified.

## Intended and out-of-scope use
- Intended: coursework on detection, segmentation and deployment.
- **Out of scope:** any clinical, diagnostic or screening use.
